  "summary": "The health-tech sector faces significant structural pressures across multiple fronts. Pharmacy benefit manager consolidation continues to deepen, with four companies now controlling 75 percent of the national market, while Medicare Advantage plans are simultaneously shifting more costs onto seniors despite stable premiums—developments that underscore ongoing concerns about healthcare affordability. Meanwhile, artificial intelligence is expanding rapidly across clinical applications beyond documentation, from chronic-care management experiments to advanced diagnostic capabilities like whole-genome analysis, though regulatory and standardization questions persist. On the biotech front, Chinese companies are rapidly gaining ground, now representing roughly half the global drug pipeline and attracting international attention, while American innovators continue advancing in targeted therapies, including gene editing and monoclonal antibody approaches. Policy uncertainty also looms as Congressional health committees face major turnover and the Trump administration pursues healthcare initiatives including scaled-back Medicare drug pricing measures that are projected to deliver substantially less savings than originally proposed. Finally, venture capital activity remains selective, as evidenced by companies like Oura delaying IPO plans amid market uncertainty while others like ElevenLabs and Ortet secure substantial valuations.",
